Take back the echo filters of an output used before

When recording restarts for another output (a switch of speakers, the sound server moving
the stream), AEC3 starts its filters from nothing and the words that come in the first
second over the new echo lose more than over the old one. A call often goes back and forth
between two outputs, and the echo path of the one used before is the same as it was.

Now, at each restart, the filters in use (refined and coarse, of both the improved and the
reference echo removal), if they converged since the last flush, are kept with the delay
they were aligned to and their ERLE estimates, in one of two slots: that of the output in
use (whose filters were taken back for it since the last restart, or whose impulse response
matches theirs at the best lag, normalized correlation of at least 0.6, with a delay up to
two blocks off; other rooms at the same delay give about 0.2), or else the one kept least
recently. The filters of every slot are tested at every restart, also after a headset, whose
filters are not kept, once the delays of both outputs have passed (4 blocks more), without
changing the filters in use; those just kept are taken back only when those of no other slot
fit (a restart can keep the output, and two outputs whose echo paths match share a slot).
The capture is correlated with the echo they estimate at their delay, at lags of up to two
blocks either way (an output can come back a few milliseconds off), and at 4 and 6 blocks
off as a control (later instead of earlier when the delay is too short to look earlier).
They fit when the capture holds that estimate at nearly its scale (0.8 to 1.25: a louder
echo, the volume raised since, would come through the suppression set for them) and not far
below it (-10 dB), the best lag is not within 16 samples of the edge of those tested, the
correlation at the best lag is at least twice that at the other delays, and no lag 16
samples or more away comes within 0.7 of it (music and other tonal sound correlates at many
lags, and then neither the lag nor the delay can be told). The test counts blocks with
capture and render and decides after 6 if the correlation is 0.9 or more, otherwise after
12, before nearend speech is likely; it follows the render it tests when the delay estimator
moves the alignment, and stops at a buffer flush or after 250 blocks with too little render
or capture.

Filters that do not fit at their delay are tested again, up to twice in the 2 s after the
restart, at a delay the delay estimator has found since the restart, when that is two blocks
or more from the one tested: an output's own buffering can move it further than the test
looks. At such a delay, to which music and other tonal sound can draw the delay estimator at
a wrong peak, no lag 16 samples or more away may come within 0.6 of the best.

On a fit, the render is aligned to their delay (or the end of the buffer, the rest shifted
within the filters), the filters are shifted by the lag found (whole blocks into the delay,
the rest within the filters, at most the two blocks their test looks either way: filters whose
echo lies further beyond the end of the buffer would not be aligned to it, and are not taken
back, the render staying where it was), they and their ERLE are taken back (partitions beyond
those kept are cleared), and the alignment is held for 250 blocks (ended by a flush or a
render underrun). Their slot then holds them as shifted, at the delay they were taken back at,
unless they were the filters just kept and fit more than two blocks from the delay they were
kept at (the echo of another output, or the same output come back further off): the slot then
keeps them as they were kept, at that delay, where they fit too. Either way they are the
filters of the output in use, kept in their slot at the next restart, never in the slot of
another output (an output come back three blocks off, as after a PulseAudio move, would
otherwise have its filters kept over those of the other output). After the hold, a delay found
within a block of it is taken for the same echo (the delay search rounds it either way), and
the render is not aligned to it: that would set the filters taken back aside, a second or so
after taking them back, in 2 to 4 of 7 changes per run with outputs up to 30 ms off. The
filters and the suppressor then leave their initial state, as they would once converged. The
initial state itself goes on until its end, 2.5 s of render later, which then keeps the ERLE
taken back instead of setting it to nothing (the words starting then would be suppressed more
than without this change); a gain change on the block they are taken back keeps it too; ending
it at the take-back cost words after a later switch to a headset. The delay the render is
aligned to is noted with every delay applied, also one where the buffer already stood (a delay
given from outside). Logs "kept filters of slot S tested, ..., fit" or "do not fit" for each
test, "kept filters of slot S tested again at delay D", "filters of delay D taken back at
block N (slot S, shifted by X samples)" after "Delay changed to D", "kept filters fit but are
not taken back" (the delay changed, or beyond the render buffer) and "kept filters tests
stopped" (no render or capture, or a flush).

(The figures below were measured before the slot of an output come back more than two blocks
off, the cap of the shift and the gain change on the take-back block were fixed; the runs
with the device's delay up to 30 ms off and with a new output's delay 8 or 16 ms longer can
differ.)
Measured in an AEC3-only simulation (16 kHz, far speech a 30 s recording, 48 runs of 8
changes in double talk, echo +1.8 dB over the words; against the same runs without this
change; dt0.5/dt3.5 are the mean loss of a word starting 0.5 s and 3.5 s after the change,
over the same stretch with no change):
- back and forth between two outputs (77 and 193 ms, other rooms): dt0.5 2.05 -> 0.38 dB,
  dt3.5 0.29 -> -0.01, words more than 6 dB down 0.9 % -> 0; another room at the same delay
  1.94 -> 0.36; another room only 1.85 -> 0.40. The device's delay off by 3 ms each time
  2.21 -> 0.94, by up to 30 ms 2.03 -> 1.23. Without the played sound being kept 2.05 ->
  0.57. From a headset back to speakers 0.72 -> 0.31. Two outputs, a headset between them
  and back (A, B, A, headset, A, B, ...; other rooms, 3 ms off): 0.85 -> 0.53, words more
  than 6 dB down 2.7 % -> 1.2 %, dt3.5 0.49 -> -0.26, lower at every change. A new output's
  delay starting 8 ms longer and settling over 0.3 s: 1.48 -> 0.36 (8 ms shorter: 1.51 ->
  0.50, 16 ms longer: 1.52 -> 0.57).
- With the previous change that keeps the played sound at a short gap: 2.07 -> 0.38 (outside
  delay 40 ms: 2.09 -> 0.38), another room at the same delay 1.94 -> 0.31, with the same
  room 1.69 -> 0.30, from a headset back to speakers 0.86 -> 0.33; music and the same output
  kept, the same to the sample.
- Of 384 words per case, none loses more than 1 dB at 0.5 s. At 3.5 s, by the error to word
  ratio, 0 or 1 lose more than 1 dB, fewer than a change of 0.01 dB in the far gain alone
  gives (4 to 11). In the sequence with a headset, which has room noise and jitter, 25 of
  336 words lose more than 1 dB at 0.5 s and 53 gain more; the change of 0.01 dB in the far
  gain alone gives 15 and 16.
- music and unmuting: the same to the sample. Echo bursts at a restart the same or lower,
  none more than 0.6 dB higher.
